    [1] What can reduce a health insurance claim — Room rent limit and proportionate deduction: Some health insurance policies cap the room rent they will pay per day, for example at one per cent of the sum insured per day, or at a particular room type such as a shared room. If the patient takes a costlier room than the limit, the insurer may cut other room-linked charges, such as doctors', surgeons' and nursing fees, in the same proportion as the room rent excess. This is called proportionate deduction, and it is one of the biggest surprises at claim time, because the cut applies to far more than the room itself. Some plans have no room rent limit, and some set a separate limit for the ICU.
    [2] What can reduce a health insurance claim — Why the sum insured is not always the amount paid: A health insurance policy with a sum insured of, say, 10 lakh rupees does not always pay a full bill up to 10 lakh rupees. Room rent limits, co-pay, deductibles, sub-limits, items the policy does not pay for, and exclusions can each reduce the amount paid. These terms are listed in the policy wording and summarised in the customer information sheet. Comparing these terms is as important as comparing the sum insured and the premium.
    [3] What can reduce a health insurance claim — Items the policy does not pay for: Every health insurance policy has a list of items it does not pay for, such as many consumables, registration and admission charges, and personal items. These amounts are paid by the patient at discharge, even in a cashless claim. This is why cashless treatment does not always mean the patient pays nothing at the hospital: non-payable items, co-pay, deductibles and any room rent difference are still the patient's to pay.
